+ ## Pre-Wiring (the real board meeting)
+ Consequential board decisions are made in 1:1 calls before the meeting; the meeting ratifies them. A director surprised in the room converts a business problem into a governance problem — blindsided directors start managing you instead of the plan.
+ Procedure:
+ - 3-7 days out: 15-30 min call with each director.
+ - Lead with the contentious item. Hear objections while you can still adapt the proposal or line up support.
+ - If a director will oppose, you want to know it on the call, not discover it in the room.
+ - 1 week before: full deck and materials sent. Day of: zero new information.
+ ## Board Meeting Structure
+ | Highlights / lowlights | 5 min | Context; confirms no surprises |
+ | Strategic topics | 30 min | The 2-3 questions you need the board's brain on |
+ | Asks | 5 min | Specific help, named |
+ Rules of the room:
+ - Assume the deck was read; never narrate slides. If directors arrive unread, shorten the deck, don't read it to them.
+ - Present problems WITH your proposed response — a problem without a plan invites the board to write one for you.
+ - End every meeting with a closed session (directors without CEO). Make it routine so it carries no signal, then call the lead director afterward for the feedback.
+ **Monthly, especially when it's hard.** The first believers in your next round are the investors who watched you report bad months honestly; silence reads as failure plus concealment.
+ Template: one-liner on company state · 3-5 metrics with trend · wins · challenges (honest) · asks (specific: intros, references, expertise) · what to watch next month.
+ Bad news travels by phone before it travels by deck — no director learns about a crisis from pre-read materials.
+ **Cash running low**: raise it while options exist — at the 12-18 month runway mark (zones in `finance.md`), not inside 6. Show cut scenarios ("cut X, runway becomes Y"), propose one plan, and ask directly about bridge appetite.
+ **Missing plan**: own the miss without hedging, show the root cause, present a recovery plan with dated milestones. Trust is rebuilt by hitting the next three milestones, not by the apology.
+ **Pivot**: frame as a conclusion from data, not a confession. Show what was falsified, present the new direction with conviction, ask for explicit endorsement — a half-endorsed pivot dies in the next hard quarter.
+ | Stage | Ideal board |
+ Board structure is negotiated at the term sheet, never after (→ `fundraising.md`). The independent seat is your tiebreaker — recruit it yourself, deliberately, before you need the vote.
+ - Updates monthly, calls quarterly minimum; relationships built before you need them.
+ - Know each investor's thesis, their fund's age, and remaining reserves — fund mechanics, not goodwill, determine who can bridge you. Ask before you need it.
+ - Ask for specific help; "any thoughts?" produces none.
+ - Don't over-rotate on the loudest voice — one director's pet metric is not the board's position.

### decisions.md  [docs]
- ## Decision Speed Matrix
- | Reversible | Irreversible |
- |------------|--------------|
- | Decide fast, 70% info | Decide carefully, 90% info |
- | Delegate widely | Escalate to CEO |
- | Bias toward action | Bias toward analysis |
- | Fail fast, learn | Get it right first time |
- **Examples:**
- - Reversible: Pricing test, new feature, hire a contractor
- - Irreversible: Major pivot, exec firing, acquisition, fundraise terms
- ## Pre-Mortem
- Before major decisions, imagine it's a year later and the decision failed spectacularly. Ask: "What went wrong?"
- Surfaces risks that optimism obscures.
- For important decisions, document:
- 1. Date and context
- 2. Options considered
- 3. Decision criteria (defined BEFORE evaluating)
- 4. What you decided and why
- 5. What would change your mind
- 6. Review date
- Prevents hindsight bias. Improves future decisions.
- ## RAPID Framework
- For complex organizational decisions, assign roles:
- | **R**ecommend | Proposes the decision |
- | **A**gree | Must sign off (veto power) |
- | **P**erform | Executes the decision |
- | **I**nput | Provides information, no veto |
- | **D**ecide | Makes final call |
- Common mistake: Too many A's = paralysis.
- ## Disagree and Commit
- When the team can't reach consensus:
- 1. Everyone voices position clearly
- 2. Decision-maker decides
- 3. Entire team commits fully
- 4. No undermining after the meeting
- Consensus is a luxury. Commitment is mandatory.
- ## Decision Hygiene
- **Before deciding:**
- - What's the ask? (clarity)
- - Is this reversible? (speed calibration)
- - Who needs to input? (completeness)
- - What's the deadline? (urgency)
- **After deciding:**
- - Who owns execution?
- - When do we review results?
- - What would change our mind?
- | Sunk cost fallacy | Ignore past investment, evaluate future only |
- | Confirmation bias | Assign someone to argue the opposite |
- | Analysis paralysis | Set decision deadlines, honor them |
- | HIPPO (highest paid person's opinion) | Last to speak, not first |
- | Recency bias | Look at longer timeframes |
+ ## Speed vs Reversibility
+ | Two-way door (reversible) | One-way door (irreversible) |
+ |---------------------------|-----------------------------|
+ | Decide at ~70% of the information you wish you had (Bezos, 2016 letter) | Slow down; run a pre-mortem; get more data |
+ | Delegate to a single owner | Escalate to CEO, often to board |
+ | Wrong call = walk back, learn | Wrong call = permanent damage |
+ | Pricing test, feature, contractor, process change | Pivot, exec exit, term sheet, layoff, acquisition |
+ The common failure is misclassification in one direction: mature orgs treat two-way doors as one-way and grind to a halt — that asymmetry, not recklessness, was Bezos's actual warning.
+ **Tiebreak when unsure on timing**: compare cost of delay to cost of error. If a week of delay costs more than a wrong-but-correctable call, decide now.
+ ## Who Decides What
+ CEO reserves three classes: one-way doors, cross-functional deadlocks, and precedent-setting values calls. Everything else gets one named owner. Escalations must arrive as options plus a recommendation — an open question escalated is delegation upward.
+ ## Pre-Mortem (Gary Klein)
+ 1. Frame: "It is 12 months later. This decision failed badly."
+ 2. Everyone writes reasons **independently and silently** before any discussion — this step blocks anchoring on the senior voice and is the entire method; skip it and you get polite agreement.
+ 3. Read out, cluster, assign mitigations to the top risks.
+ 4. Decide with the risks priced in.
+ For consequential decisions, record: date and context; options considered; criteria (defined BEFORE evaluating options); the decision and why; what would change your mind; a review date.
+ On the review date, grade the **process**, not the outcome — good decisions lose sometimes and bad ones win (resulting, Annie Duke). Grading outcomes teaches your team to hide risk.
+ ## RAPID (Bain)
+ | **R**ecommend | Builds the proposal |
+ | **A**gree | Must sign off — real veto |
+ | **P**erform | Executes |
+ | **I**nput | Consulted, no veto |
+ | **D**ecide | One name. Not a committee. |
+ Failure modes: more than one name in D (no decision), a chain of A's (veto paralysis), and Input-holders who believed they were A's — announce the roles before the debate, not after, or the "betrayed" I's undermine execution.
+ ## Disagree and Commit (Grove/Intel)
+ 1. Everyone states their position with reasons — dissent is mandatory before the call, prohibited after.
+ 2. Decision-maker decides and closes explicitly: "I heard X and Y. We're doing Z. What would change my mind: W."
+ 3. Full team commits; no relitigating in side channels.
+ The stated change-my-mind condition is what makes commitment honest — without it, dissenters wait for failure instead of watching for W.
+ | Sunk cost | Evaluate only future costs and returns; past spend is gone either way |
+ | Confirmation bias | Assign a named person to argue the opposite before you decide |
+ | Analysis paralysis | Set a decision deadline with a default action if it passes |
+ | HIPPO (highest-paid person's opinion) | CEO speaks last in the debate, first only in the announcement |
+ | Quick consensus | On big bets, fast agreement means the debate never happened — force the pre-mortem |
+ | Recency | Zoom the timeframe out one order of magnitude before deciding |

